+\subsection{Detection, alignment, and worst-case certification}
+\label{sec:objectives}
+
+The word ``minimum'' describes different objects in alignment and
+certification. We distinguish the original SWAN detector, AliMark's
+reconstruction/alignment detector, and our finite-set evaluation.
+Table~\ref{tab:objectives} compares their inputs, objectives, and guarantees.
+This is a methodological comparison; we do not report matched SWAN--AliMark
+performance or certify AliMark.
+
+\paragraph{SWAN: scoring the supplied units.}
+For a fixed segmentation $P$, SWAN marks each unit whose maximum S2Match
+against the secret bank crosses the unit threshold, then aggregates the hit
+count with Equation~\ref{eq:swan} \citep{ye2026swan}. Its maximum is over AMR
+templates for one unit. The original document detector contains neither
+boundary-search DP nor alignment to a secret sequence. It evaluates the
+units returned by its segmenter.
+
+\paragraph{AliMark: minimizing block-edit cost to recover evidence.}
+Let $\mathcal R(X)$ denote the candidates in AliMark's original
+Re-Structurer. For an input of $N$ sentences, these are nominally $2N$
+candidates: the unchanged text, $N-1$ single adjacent merges, and $N$
+single midpoint splits. They are deliberately limited to one operation
+\citep[Section~3.2.1]{li2026alimark}. A candidate $P$ has $n(P)$ extracted
+blocks $b_P^1,\ldots,b_P^{n(P)}$, each of $M$ bits. The Adaptive Bit Sequence
+Alignment module compares them with $\ell$ blocks of the secret sequence,
+where $\ell$ varies over a declared candidate set $\mathcal L(P)$.
+The reconstructions can change unit count; $\mathcal R(X)$ is not our
+fixed-$N$ relocation set $\mathcal A_k$.
+
+For each pair $(P,\ell)$, AliMark's Equation~(4) uses the block-edit DP
+\begin{equation}
+H_{P,\ell}(u,v)=\min\left\{
+\begin{array}{l}
+H_{P,\ell}(u-1,v)+M,\\
+H_{P,\ell}(u,v-1)+M,\\
+H_{P,\ell}(u-1,v-1)+\operatorname{Ham}(b_P^u,s^v)
+\end{array}\right\}.
+\label{eq:alimark-dp}
+\end{equation}
+The boundary costs are $H(u,0)=uM$ and $H(0,v)=vM$. Its block edit rate is
+\begin{equation}
+r(P,\ell)=\frac{H_{P,\ell}(n(P),\ell)}
+                   {M\max\{n(P),\ell\}}.
+\end{equation}
+Using the original paper's length-indexed null mean and standard deviation,
+the final detector is
+\begin{equation}
+\mathcal Z_{\mathrm{Ali}}(X)=
+\max_{\substack{P\in\mathcal R(X)\\ \ell\in\mathcal L(P)}}
+\frac{\bar\mu_{M,n(P)}-r(P,\ell)}{\bar\sigma_{M,n(P)}}.
+\label{eq:alimark-outer}
+\end{equation}
+Equations~\ref{eq:alimark-dp}--\ref{eq:alimark-outer} restate AliMark's
+Equations~(4)--(6), with renamed indices \citep{li2026alimark}. For fixed
+$(P,\ell)$, DP finds the least-cost alignment; the outer maximum selects the
+strongest normalized evidence. Because normalization varies with candidate
+length, that outer maximum need not equal a single global minimum of
+unnormalized edit cost. Its decision asks whether a reconstruction and an
+alignment provide sufficient evidence. An optimal alignment does not imply
+that every possible text partition was searched or remains detectable.
+
+\paragraph{Our evaluation: minimizing the detector score.}
+Here the original token tape is fixed, each DP path constructs a partition
+in $\mathcal A_k(x,P_0)$, and the output is
+\begin{equation}
+C_k(x)=\min_{P\in\mathcal A_k(x,P_0)}D(x,P).
+\label{eq:objective-min}
+\end{equation}
+For a nonempty finite threat set and a fixed threshold, the implication
+$C_k(x)>\tau \Rightarrow \forall P\in\mathcal A_k,\ D(x,P)>\tau$
+follows from the definition of a minimum. The substantive algorithmic
+obligation is to show that the implemented DP returns exactly that minimum.
+It chooses text boundaries and, for adjacent-span scores, retains the
+previous span. It does not align extracted bit blocks to a secret sequence.
+AliMark's higher-is-better aggregate in Equation~\ref{eq:alimark-outer}
+includes candidate selection and alignment; it is not a locally
+decomposable $D$ covered by the DP classes below without another derivation.
+
